\fs32\b Directives\b0\fs20\par
\par
Directives are special lines which provide additional information or instructions within a transcription. They are not ordinary index entries and cannot be typed directly into the transcription grid.\par
\par
WinBMD2 supports three directives:\par
\par
\b #COMMENT\b0\par
Used when the transcription differs in some way from what appears in the index. For example, a comment may explain an alteration, deletion or annotation visible on the scan.\par
\par
\b #THEORY\b0\par
Used when the index has been transcribed as shown, but there is good reason to believe that the information in the index itself is incorrect. The reason for the theory should be included.\par
\par
\b +PAGE\b0\par
Used to indicate a change of page within the transcription. These are very unusual except that the last line MUST be a +PAGE directive.\par
\par
\fs24\b Entering a directive\b0\fs20\par
Directives cannot be entered by simply typing #COMMENT, #THEORY or +PAGE into a grid cell.\par
\par
To enter a directive, use the directive command in WinBMD2. The Directive Editor will open and allow you to select the required directive and enter the information associated with it. When the editor is closed, WinBMD2 inserts the directive into the transcription at the appropriate position.\par
\par
\fs24\b Further information\b0\fs20\par
FreeBMD provides more detailed guidance on the correct use of #COMMENT and #THEORY, including examples of when each should and should not be used.\par
\par
https://www.freebmd.org.uk/comments-help.html\par
